WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.120
So we are looking at this incredible tension today,

00:03.320 --> 00:09.140
something that sits right at the bleeding edge of artificial intelligence and, well, planetary physics.

00:09.400 --> 00:12.740
Yeah, it really is a fundamental clash of two different worlds, honestly.

00:13.100 --> 00:18.260
Right, because on one side you have the AI community, and they are celebrating this massive breakthrough,

00:18.520 --> 00:25.060
an AI that simulates global weather systems almost 62 times faster than our most advanced supercomputers.

00:25.080 --> 00:29.500
Which is an astonishing number. I mean, from an engineering perspective, it is a triumph.

00:29.500 --> 00:34.840
It is. But then, on the other side, you have this rigorous mathematical proof.

00:35.160 --> 00:43.560
And it basically demonstrates that this exact same AI model is fundamentally and silently deleting the world's oceans.

00:43.800 --> 00:49.480
Yeah. Deleting mass, violating conservation laws, just, you know, completely falling apart under the hood.

00:49.780 --> 00:52.760
So today's deep dive is all about that contradiction.

00:53.140 --> 00:59.260
What actually happens when a machine learning model achieves perfect accuracy in a small local area,

00:59.260 --> 01:03.620
but just collapses reality when you ask it to simulate an entire planet.

01:03.920 --> 01:06.740
Right. Local perfection versus global failure.

01:06.880 --> 01:15.340
Exactly. And we are pulling this apart using a highly technical forensic 2026 essay by the independent researcher Flyxion.

01:15.600 --> 01:23.080
It is titled Local Transitions and Global Admissibility, Composing Learned Atmospheric Update Operators.

01:23.080 --> 01:26.520
And I just have to say, it is an incredibly consequential piece of literature.

01:27.020 --> 01:34.240
Because, well, it forces us to completely reevaluate how we validate AI simulations of complex physical systems.

01:34.360 --> 01:35.800
It is not just about the weather, right?

01:36.040 --> 01:36.960
No, not at all.

01:37.740 --> 01:42.240
Flyxion's critique focuses on a specific AI atmospheric model called strata,

01:42.240 --> 01:45.460
which was developed by Hugh and a large team of researchers.

01:46.260 --> 01:53.220
But the mathematical proofs, the real epistemic questions raised here, they extend far beyond atmospheric science.

01:53.700 --> 01:55.280
Oh, wow. So where else does this apply?

01:55.560 --> 01:56.600
Well, any domain, really.

01:56.780 --> 01:57.020
Okay.

01:57.140 --> 01:59.320
Economics, fluid dynamics, epidemiology.

01:59.720 --> 02:06.340
Anywhere we attempt to capture macro-level reality by having a neural network, you know, memorize micro-level transitions.

02:06.340 --> 02:12.180
Right. And for those of you listening, whether you build these models or manage tech infrastructure

02:12.180 --> 02:15.540
or just want to understand the actual limits of AI prediction,

02:15.800 --> 02:21.040
this deep dive is going to fundamentally change how you interpret the phrase highly accurate model.

02:21.620 --> 02:24.380
Yeah, you will never look at those press releases the same way again.

02:24.920 --> 02:29.860
Because as Flyxion argues, highly accurate in the machine learning sense might just mean

02:29.860 --> 02:36.180
producing a highly convincing sequence of events in a physically impossible universe.

02:36.340 --> 02:38.000
That is a great way to put it.

02:38.400 --> 02:42.080
To visualize this, I want you to think of a hyper-realistic patchwork quilt.

02:42.340 --> 02:46.160
You inspect one single square fabric and it is just a masterpiece.

02:46.580 --> 02:47.980
Right. The details are perfect.

02:48.140 --> 02:53.480
Exactly. The stitching, the pattern, it flawlessly represents its local patch of reality.

02:53.900 --> 03:00.100
But the real question is, does stitching those perfect squares together actually guarantee a functioning blanket?

03:00.660 --> 03:03.760
Or does the quilt tear at the seams the moment you apply tension?

03:03.760 --> 03:08.100
Because the threads connecting the patches don't actually obey the laws of physics.

03:08.860 --> 03:14.360
Right. That tension between the perfection of the local patch and the structural integrity of the global manifold,

03:14.640 --> 03:17.840
that is the precise architectural vulnerability of strata.

03:18.100 --> 03:23.520
So before we get to the vulnerability, we really need to understand what strata actually achieved.

03:23.900 --> 03:25.700
Right. We have to give them credit where it is due.

03:26.100 --> 03:31.060
From a purely engineering and computational standpoint, it is a monumental piece of architecture.

03:31.060 --> 03:33.340
So let's get into the mechanics of it.

03:33.760 --> 03:37.560
Strata is an autoregressive transformer emulator.

03:38.060 --> 03:42.060
And it is emulating a kilometer-scale global atmospheric model.

03:43.120 --> 03:44.140
Which is incredibly hard to do.

03:44.520 --> 03:50.880
Yeah. The traditional approach to simulating the atmosphere involves these massive high-performance computing clusters.

03:51.580 --> 03:58.300
Supercomputers running numerical models that calculate fluid dynamics and thermodynamics for the entire globe simultaneously.

03:58.300 --> 04:07.220
And just to get some scale, the reference model that strata is trying to emulate operates on a grid with roughly 25 million horizontal cells.

04:07.540 --> 04:08.660
25 million.

04:09.020 --> 04:18.260
Yeah. And trying to train an attention-based neural network to predict all 25 million cells simultaneously, well, it is computationally intractable.

04:18.780 --> 04:20.340
Because of how transformers work, right?

04:20.340 --> 04:25.900
Exactly. Transformer attention mechanisms generally scale quadratically with sequence length.

04:26.440 --> 04:36.060
So, you know, you cannot just feed an AI a sequence of 25 million tokens representing the entire Earth's atmosphere and expect it to compute a forward transition efficiently.

04:36.280 --> 04:36.880
You just choke.

04:37.340 --> 04:40.200
So the breakthrough with strata is its core architectural maneuver.

04:40.800 --> 04:42.920
Trained locally, deployed globally.

04:43.180 --> 04:43.400
Right.

04:43.400 --> 04:49.600
Which means, instead of swallowing the whole planet at once, it learns on local spatial tiles.

04:49.740 --> 04:53.840
And the paper specifies tiles of about 576 kilometers across.

04:54.220 --> 04:55.900
Yeah, that is the magic number they landed on.

04:56.220 --> 04:58.360
So the model takes the state of one of these tiles.

04:58.360 --> 05:07.380
You know, the wind vectors, the temperature profiles, the specific humidity across 24 vertical levels, along with the external forcing like sunlight.

05:07.380 --> 05:13.100
Right. And it predicts the exact state of that 576 kilometer tile exactly 10 minutes later.

05:13.460 --> 05:15.760
10 minutes. That is a time step.

05:15.980 --> 05:18.180
Right. It is learning a local transition rule.

05:18.640 --> 05:23.480
State X at time T plus forcing equals state X at time T plus 10 minutes.

05:23.720 --> 05:27.440
And the data it is learning from is highly granular, isn't it?

05:27.560 --> 05:27.980
Oh, very.

05:27.980 --> 05:35.580
It uses a storm-resolving model with an effective grid spacing of 3.3 kilometers, archived onto a 4.9 kilometer grid.

05:35.960 --> 05:40.780
So we are talking about multi-scale, high-resolution slices of the troposphere and stratosphere.

05:40.780 --> 05:44.840
The computational efficiency they extract from this localized approach is staggering.

05:45.560 --> 05:51.500
I mean, the paper reports that strata achieves 48.1 simulated days per megawatt hour.

05:51.600 --> 05:53.860
That is on a cluster of 16 H100 GPUs, yeah.

05:53.860 --> 06:02.100
Right. And if you compare that to the reference numerical model, the supercomputer, that only gets 0.78 simulated days per megawatt hour.

06:02.180 --> 06:03.700
Which is just a massive difference.

06:03.880 --> 06:07.220
Yeah, you are looking at a speed-up ratio of nearly 62.

06:07.700 --> 06:12.380
The maximum throughput is 741 simulated days per wall clock day.

06:12.380 --> 06:21.380
Think about that. You can generate two years of high-resolution planetary weather on a standard GPU cluster over your lunch break.

06:21.640 --> 06:23.020
That is insane.

06:23.420 --> 06:27.280
Which is exactly why the AI community has just embraced this architecture wholeheartedly.

06:27.920 --> 06:32.680
The marginal cost of producing a trajectory drops by orders of magnitude.

06:33.080 --> 06:36.380
But we need to look closely at the spatial processing that enables us, right?

06:36.380 --> 06:44.860
We do. Because to run the exact same neural network weights on any tile anywhere on the Earth, the model has to be spatially invariant.

06:45.140 --> 06:50.000
Meaning it has to treat a tile in Paris the same way it treats a tile in the Sahara.

06:50.440 --> 06:50.960
Exactly.

06:51.400 --> 06:57.160
But a standard latitude-longitude grid introduces severe geometric distortions, particularly near the poles.

06:57.640 --> 06:59.740
Right, because of the converging meridians.

06:59.740 --> 07:07.540
A weather system near the Arctic Circle is physically distorted on a flat latitude-longitude map compared to an identical system at the equator.

07:07.920 --> 07:08.780
You get stretched out.

07:09.160 --> 07:09.300
Yeah.

07:09.540 --> 07:13.100
So to get around this, Strata implements a stereographic variant.

07:13.660 --> 07:17.560
They couple it with rotary-position embeddings and neighborhood attention.

07:18.540 --> 07:21.000
Okay. Unpack that stereographic part for us.

07:21.380 --> 07:28.100
They essentially project the local atmospheric state onto a flat tangent plane that is centered exactly on the tile.

07:28.100 --> 07:39.860
And by rotating the wind vector components relative to a local north, a physically identical storm at the equator and at the Arctic are presented to the neural network with the exact same structural geometry.

07:40.660 --> 07:43.260
Oh, I see. So it doesn't get confused by the curve of the Earth.

07:43.500 --> 07:43.780
Right.

07:44.620 --> 07:50.860
It is a really elegant way to bypass the singularity at the poles and enforce that translational invariance.

07:50.860 --> 07:59.740
The neural network just learns a universal rule for atmospheric fluid dynamics without needing to dedicate parameters to untangling the geometry of a sphere.

08:00.220 --> 08:02.300
It just evaluates the local flat plane.

08:02.620 --> 08:04.220
But I want to push back on something here.

08:04.560 --> 08:06.980
The explicit choice of the tile size.

08:07.480 --> 08:09.740
They settled on 576 kilometers.

08:10.100 --> 08:11.940
Why 576 kilometers?

08:12.120 --> 08:13.340
Why not bigger or smaller?

08:13.340 --> 08:18.100
Well, if you are chopping up the global domain, that boundary size isn't arbitrary.

08:19.000 --> 08:27.020
The paper actually defends this using a strict physical heuristic, which is based on the speed of sound and their specific integration time step.

08:27.380 --> 08:28.160
The 10 minutes.

08:28.400 --> 08:28.840
Precisely.

08:29.200 --> 08:37.720
In 10 minutes, which is the time step the AI is predicting, acoustic signals, which are the fastest propagating relevant physical waves in the atmosphere,

08:38.460 --> 08:40.860
well, they travel approximately 200 kilometers.

08:41.200 --> 08:43.060
Wait. Acoustic signals?

08:43.060 --> 08:44.300
Yeah, like sound.

08:44.780 --> 08:44.900
Yeah.

08:45.220 --> 08:49.500
In fluid dynamics, the propagation of pressure waves happens at the speed of sound.

08:49.900 --> 08:54.820
So if you are looking at the exact center of a 576-kilometer tile,

08:55.500 --> 09:01.960
no physical information from outside the boundaries of that tile can possibly reach the center within a 10-minute window.

09:02.480 --> 09:05.740
Because it would have to travel more than 200 kilometers to get there.

09:05.860 --> 09:06.240
Exactly.

09:06.240 --> 09:13.100
The tile contains all the causal information necessary to compute the forward state for that specific center point.

09:13.560 --> 09:13.840
Okay.

09:14.140 --> 09:21.620
So locality, in that strictly bounded sense, is a physically sound heuristic for a single isolated forward step.

09:21.620 --> 09:22.120
It is.

09:22.120 --> 09:26.560
But this is exactly where Flyxion introduces the central conflict of the essay.

09:27.180 --> 09:36.860
Because there is a profound mathematical distinction between the locality of a single time increment and the locality of a continuous trajectory.

09:36.860 --> 09:47.320
Because predicting 10 minutes in isolation is a completely different physical regime than stitching those 10-minute predictions together auto-aggressively, you know, to simulate a month or a year.

09:47.320 --> 09:47.940
Right.

09:48.660 --> 09:52.400
Over a single step, the causal cone is strictly bounded by the acoustic speed.

09:53.060 --> 09:57.360
But over hundreds of sequential steps, that causal cone expands indefinitely.

09:57.880 --> 09:59.000
It bleeds over the edges.

09:59.280 --> 09:59.740
Exactly.

10:00.640 --> 10:05.700
A localized disturbance propagates across the boundaries, interacting with the planetary scale circulation.

10:06.280 --> 10:10.980
The accumulated sequence of local steps eventually couples to the global manifold.

10:11.460 --> 10:14.960
And this forces us to ask a really uncomfortable question.

10:15.420 --> 10:16.000
Which is?

10:16.000 --> 10:21.280
What is the neural network actually learning to do when it calculates that local step?

10:21.740 --> 10:25.460
Is it learning real weather or just a video game version of it?

10:25.800 --> 10:29.340
Which brings us to what Flyxion calls the emulation illusion.

10:29.740 --> 10:34.700
When we look at strata, there's this latent assumption that it is learning the physics of the atmosphere.

10:35.180 --> 10:38.640
But Flyxion makes a critical epistemic clarification here.

10:39.220 --> 10:40.240
Yeah, and it is a big one.

10:41.020 --> 10:44.820
Strata is an emulator of a numerical reference model.

10:44.820 --> 10:47.980
It is not an emulator of the Earth's actual atmosphere.

10:48.640 --> 10:48.760
Right.

10:49.240 --> 10:53.880
If we denote the reference supercomputer simulation as an operator, let's call it F,

10:54.480 --> 11:03.000
the objective function of the neural network, F theta, is just to minimize the divergence between its output and the output of F.

11:03.000 --> 11:07.920
So the learning problem is F theta approximately equals F.

11:08.160 --> 11:08.560
Exactly.

11:09.120 --> 11:11.500
It is entirely divorced from reality.

11:11.820 --> 11:13.540
It is just trying to copy the supercomputer.

11:13.540 --> 11:20.360
This bifurcates the whole scientific validation process into two distinct and honestly often conflated problems.

11:20.640 --> 11:26.960
The first problem is whether the reference model F actually represents the physical atmosphere accurately.

11:26.960 --> 11:29.320
Which is hard enough on its own.

11:29.620 --> 11:29.720
Right.

11:30.180 --> 11:36.020
And the second problem is whether the AI operator F theta faithfully reproduces the behavior of F.

11:36.400 --> 11:40.880
Even if the AI matches the supercomputer flawlessly, you know, with zero loss,

11:41.020 --> 11:45.080
it tells us absolutely nothing about the physical accuracy of the underlying numerical model.

11:45.080 --> 11:52.980
And that underlying numerical model, the one it's copying, is riddled with severe approximations.

11:53.200 --> 11:54.280
Because it has to be, right?

11:54.380 --> 11:55.820
Even supercomputers have limits.

11:56.080 --> 11:56.440
Exactly.

11:56.920 --> 12:01.160
At a 3.3 kilometer resolution, a simulation is considered storm resolving.

12:01.500 --> 12:06.200
But it still relies heavily on parameterizations for subgrid processes.

12:06.700 --> 12:10.080
Things that are too small for the 3.3 kilometer grid to actually see.

12:10.080 --> 12:10.560
Right.

12:11.240 --> 12:19.400
Things like cloud microphysics, the aerosol effects on radiation, boundary layer turbulence, surface fluxes of heat and moisture.

12:19.860 --> 12:24.520
None of these are explicitly resolved by the fundamental physics equations on that grid.

12:25.060 --> 12:28.620
They are approximated using heuristic parameterization schemes.

12:29.040 --> 12:32.260
They're essentially educated guesses coded into the reference model.

12:32.500 --> 12:32.780
Yes.

12:32.780 --> 12:40.160
So when the AI trains on this data, it isn't discovering the fundamental laws of thermodynamics or fluid dynamics.

12:40.500 --> 12:45.280
It is literally just curve fitting to the biases and structural assumptions of those parameterizations.

12:45.940 --> 12:50.280
So the prospect of utilizing an emulator like strata for century scale climate projection,

12:50.520 --> 12:53.780
which the original paper explicitly frames as a long-term goal,

12:54.220 --> 12:57.780
that requires an epistemic leap that the architecture simply cannot support.

12:58.560 --> 12:59.840
It is a massive leap.

12:59.840 --> 13:06.260
And the problem of what the model is actually learning is compounded massively by the scale of the training data.

13:06.460 --> 13:07.560
Oh, this part blew my mind.

13:07.880 --> 13:07.980
Yeah.

13:08.100 --> 13:15.800
So the data set used to train strata consists of only 17 days of global data from the reference model.

13:16.080 --> 13:17.860
Wait, 17 days.

13:18.000 --> 13:21.400
How can an AI learn the whole planet's weather in just 17 days?

13:21.820 --> 13:22.340
I know, right?

13:22.460 --> 13:23.200
It sounds impossible.

13:23.720 --> 13:28.520
And this is a phenomenal point of misdirection in how AI capabilities are often communicated.

13:28.520 --> 13:34.640
If you read the headline statistics, you see claims of training on millions, perhaps billions of data points.

13:35.300 --> 13:42.040
Because they extract massive quantities of those 576-kilometer tiles from the global snapshots over those 17 days.

13:42.220 --> 13:42.580
Exactly.

13:43.180 --> 13:48.740
So the raw token count, the sheer number of tiles fed into the machine, is astronomical.

13:48.740 --> 13:51.100
But the effect of sample size is what really matters.

13:51.480 --> 13:51.620
Right.

13:52.320 --> 13:54.780
The effect of degrees of freedom are exceptionally small.

13:55.380 --> 13:58.480
Atmospheric states are heavily correlated in both space and time.

13:58.800 --> 14:04.100
If you sample a tile over the North Atlantic, and then sample another tile adjacent to it 10 minutes later,

14:04.540 --> 14:07.840
you are essentially sampling the exact same synoptic weather system.

14:08.520 --> 14:10.960
You are not extracting novel physical information.

14:10.960 --> 14:11.560
No.

14:12.160 --> 14:15.260
You are just oversampling a highly correlated manifold.

14:15.940 --> 14:17.960
The informational diversity is actually negligible.

14:18.740 --> 14:24.660
And for those listening, think about how often we see impressive AI stats like that, trained on billions of data points.

14:25.240 --> 14:31.740
But if those points are just highly correlated, redundant snapshots, the AI isn't actually learning as much as you think.

14:31.740 --> 14:39.700
Flyxion highlights this to contrast the difference between an AI learning a shared local mechanism versus learning a global distribution.

14:40.160 --> 14:41.220
Learning a local mechanism.

14:41.720 --> 14:41.900
Yeah.

14:42.140 --> 14:44.380
That is achievable in 17 days.

14:44.920 --> 14:50.020
The AI can analyze enough local tiles to deduce the short-term kinematic relationships.

14:50.560 --> 14:56.600
Like how a specific gradient in the pressure field pushes a moisture plume over a 10-minute horizon.

14:57.000 --> 14:59.460
It can learn how the wind blows a cloud locally.

14:59.460 --> 15:05.440
Right. But learning a global distribution involves capturing planetary-scale variability.

15:06.300 --> 15:14.860
The El Nino-Southern Oscillation, the Madden-Julian Oscillation, blocking highs that persist for weeks and alter seasonal precipitation patterns.

15:15.220 --> 15:21.460
You cannot deduce multi-year cycles or rare extreme events from a 17-day heavily correlated sample.

15:21.660 --> 15:22.520
You just can't.

15:22.560 --> 15:25.860
Which logically constrains the operational validity of the model.

15:25.860 --> 15:35.740
The evaluation documented in the Strata paper is strictly limited to six held-out initializations, rolling out for a horizon of only 24 hours.

15:36.000 --> 15:36.400
24 hours.

15:36.560 --> 15:36.740
Yeah.

15:37.560 --> 15:42.540
And as Flyxion notes, this empirical evidence supports a claim of useful, short-range behavior.

15:43.080 --> 15:48.140
It categorically does not support claims of long-term stability or physical fidelity.

15:48.140 --> 15:54.660
So what actually happens to the physics when we let this simulation run beyond that 24-hour horizon?

15:55.200 --> 16:02.460
Because if the network has only learned the local 10-minute mechanics from a 17-day sample, what occurs on day 5 or day 30?

16:03.120 --> 16:03.800
It gets weird.

16:04.380 --> 16:10.380
Flyxion analyzes this by looking at the large-scale circulation defects that emerge over extended rollouts.

16:10.380 --> 16:14.160
It creates what they term convincing weather in the wrong world.

16:14.820 --> 16:16.640
Convincing weather in the wrong world.

16:16.700 --> 16:17.700
I love that phrasing.

16:18.000 --> 16:19.040
It is so accurate.

16:19.180 --> 16:19.340
Yeah.

16:20.000 --> 16:27.320
Because when Strata is unrolled past its training horizon, the localized metrics often maintain a deceptive plausibility.

16:28.160 --> 16:32.420
The precipitation fields continue to display organized propagating convection.

16:32.860 --> 16:38.320
So if you just look at a localized animation on a screen, it appears to be a physically coherent rainstorm.

16:38.480 --> 16:38.680
Yeah.

16:38.880 --> 16:39.560
It looks beautiful.

16:39.560 --> 16:44.440
But the planetary scale diagnostics reveal a systemic collapse.

16:45.080 --> 16:53.560
For instance, the global mean precipitation, which is the integrated mass of water falling across the entire planet, exhibits a low bias of up to 20%.

16:54.100 --> 16:57.200
You are losing a fifth of the planetary water cycle.

16:57.500 --> 16:58.120
It's just gone.

16:58.800 --> 17:04.340
Furthermore, the spectral power at length scales spanning from 100 to 2,000 kilometers is severely suppressed.

17:04.840 --> 17:09.340
And the suppression of spectral power at those specific scales is a critical failure mode.

17:09.560 --> 17:10.940
Why those scales specifically?

17:10.940 --> 17:16.660
Well, in atmospheric dynamics, energy cascades across different spatial scales.

17:17.340 --> 17:22.680
The 100 to 2,000 kilometer range represents the synoptic to mesoscale transition.

17:23.160 --> 17:30.160
It encompasses the typical extratropical cyclones and significant frontal systems that dominate global weather.

17:30.160 --> 17:32.860
The big stuff that actually moves our weather around.

17:33.140 --> 17:33.580
Exactly.

17:34.460 --> 17:43.720
When the spectral power in that band is damped down by the AI, the network is artificially destroying the kinetic energy of the planet's major weather systems.

17:43.720 --> 17:48.320
So we can ground this mathematically using a state decomposition that Flyxion provides.

17:48.840 --> 17:57.500
If we denote the total atmospheric state as X, we can decompose it into large-scale components, XL, and small-scale components, XS.

17:58.040 --> 18:00.100
So X equals XL plus XS.

18:00.600 --> 18:04.620
And a multiscale physical model must accurately integrate the evolution of both domains.

18:04.620 --> 18:09.800
But more importantly, it must correctly compute the nonlinear feedback between them.

18:10.300 --> 18:12.720
The upscale and downscale energy cascades.

18:13.480 --> 18:13.780
Right.

18:14.100 --> 18:25.580
How the localized convection, XS, aggregates to influence the large-scale jet stream, XL, and conversely, how the jet stream dictates the boundary conditions for the next localized convective event.

18:25.580 --> 18:41.020
It's a loop. But when an AI emulator accurately reproduces XS, generating plausible-looking local clouds, while simultaneously allowing XL to drift out of physical bounds and suppress that synoptic energy, the fundamental feedback loop is broken.

18:41.600 --> 18:42.380
Completely broken.

18:42.900 --> 18:49.080
It's like, imagine a movie set where the individual bricks on the buildings look incredibly realistic. They are perfect bricks.

18:49.080 --> 18:57.680
But if you step back, the entire city is tilted on a 45-degree angle. The local details are convincing, but the global structure is absurd.

18:58.420 --> 18:59.360
That is a great analogy.

19:00.080 --> 19:06.700
The resulting simulation produces locally convincing weather, embedded within a planetary state that is entirely non-physical.

19:07.200 --> 19:15.240
Now, the authors of Strata, they aren't blind. They recognize these massive, large-scale drifts. And to counteract them, they suggested an architectural patch.

19:15.240 --> 19:23.160
Yeah, they proposed coupling their high-resolution, kilometer-scale AI emulator to a separate, coarse-resolution global emulator.

19:23.420 --> 19:31.900
So the idea being that a separate AI network handles the large-scale XL constraints, while Strata fills in the XS details.

19:32.300 --> 19:42.360
But Fragomen points out that combining two independent learned operators, without rigorously suppressing their interaction terms, it introduces massive mathematical peril.

19:42.800 --> 19:44.300
Because they step on each other's toes.

19:44.300 --> 19:50.140
Exactly. In a continuous physical spectrum, the separation of scales is rarely clean.

19:50.880 --> 20:01.340
If both the coarse model and the fine model are predicting kinetic energy fluxes in overlapping spectral bands, you almost inevitably double-count the energy or create spurious numerical wave interactions.

20:01.800 --> 20:05.480
You end up with two completely different operators fighting over the same physical variables.

20:05.820 --> 20:06.040
Right.

20:06.040 --> 20:08.640
But the interventions didn't stop at theoretical coupling.

20:08.840 --> 20:16.960
To force Strata into a usable state just for those 24-hour rollouts, the engineers implemented direct code-level modifications.

20:17.380 --> 20:19.680
They applied multi-step fine-tuning.

20:20.060 --> 20:22.180
They altered the decoder architecture.

20:22.180 --> 20:28.420
They utilized spectral filtering on the large-scale vertical velocity to smooth out unstable air currents.

20:28.420 --> 20:34.520
And they applied ad hoc corrections for negative-specific humidity and negative precipitation.

20:35.060 --> 20:38.640
They explicitly clipped the variables to enforce non-negativity constraints.

20:39.220 --> 20:40.840
QV is greater than or equal to zero.

20:41.220 --> 20:43.440
Anyway, so what does this all mean?

20:43.600 --> 20:44.600
I want to push back here.

20:44.600 --> 20:49.740
If they clip negative humidity to zero, isn't that just fixing the problem?

20:49.860 --> 20:51.160
I mean, you can't have negative water.

20:51.960 --> 20:59.420
Well, this is where Flyxion introduces a really vital philosophical framework for evaluating AI in the physical sciences.

20:59.960 --> 21:05.320
When an AI predicts negative 5% humidity, which obviously violates the laws of physics,

21:05.920 --> 21:10.800
and an engineer writes a post-processing script that just clips any negative value to zero,

21:10.800 --> 21:14.140
we have to ask, what is mathematically happening to the system?

21:14.980 --> 21:15.180
Right.

21:16.120 --> 21:20.140
Flyxion separates three properties that the AI community constantly conflates.

21:21.100 --> 21:26.560
Uniform boundedness, constraint satisfaction, and physical fidelity.

21:26.820 --> 21:27.940
Okay, let's break those down.

21:29.100 --> 21:32.180
Clipping negative humidity strictly falls under constraint satisfaction.

21:32.740 --> 21:35.940
You are enforcing the algebraic rule that water mass cannot be negative.

21:35.940 --> 21:42.040
But satisfying that constraint artificially completely destroys the physical fidelity of the simulation.

21:42.740 --> 21:45.820
Because if the neural network calculated a negative mass in one grid cell,

21:45.900 --> 21:51.520
it almost certainly erroneously advected too much positive mass into an adjacent grid cell.

21:51.780 --> 21:52.280
Exactly.

21:52.900 --> 21:56.240
By intervening and arbitrarily changing the negative number to zero,

21:56.720 --> 21:58.520
you aren't balancing the continuous equation.

21:59.000 --> 22:03.780
You are artificially injecting mass into a closed system just to mask a local calculation error.

22:03.780 --> 22:07.160
You are permanently corrupting the global conservation budgets.

22:07.540 --> 22:10.700
Water is magically being created out of nowhere to fix a math error.

22:11.000 --> 22:11.200
Yes.

22:11.740 --> 22:13.200
The constraint is satisfied locally,

22:13.780 --> 22:17.180
but the planetary water budget is fundamentally violated.

22:18.060 --> 22:23.000
And Flyxion extends this critique to the spectral filtering of the vertical velocity field as well.

22:23.420 --> 22:28.240
Right, because when the vertical velocity begins to exhibit high-frequency numerical instability,

22:28.600 --> 22:32.200
applying a spectral filter smooths the field and removes the symptom.

22:32.200 --> 22:38.180
But it does absolutely nothing to correct the underlying linear propagator matrix in the neural network

22:38.180 --> 22:41.320
that is actually generating eigenvalues greater than one.

22:41.840 --> 22:44.960
The AI is still actively trying to force the system into instability.

22:45.380 --> 22:49.600
The filter is merely throwing away the energy at every time step to prevent a crash.

22:49.960 --> 22:54.760
And this highlights the critical distinction between uniform boundedness and physical fidelity.

22:54.760 --> 23:02.280
Boundedness merely implies that the L2 norm of the state variables does not explode to infinity over a designated time horizon.

23:02.560 --> 23:02.820
Right.

23:03.000 --> 23:06.980
Surviving a 24-hour rollout, which is the metric of success in the strata paper,

23:07.520 --> 23:09.760
only proves finite horizon boundedness.

23:10.660 --> 23:15.420
It just demonstrates that the numerical blowup takes longer than 24 hours to manifest visibly.

23:15.620 --> 23:16.020
Exactly.

23:16.020 --> 23:21.700
It provides absolutely no mathematical proof that the model is uniformly bounded over long integrations,

23:21.960 --> 23:25.620
let alone that the trajectory remains on the correct physical manifold.

23:25.860 --> 23:29.300
Which is wild, because in traditional computational fluid dynamics,

23:29.500 --> 23:34.360
there is a dedicated field called structure-preserving numerical integration, right?

23:34.360 --> 23:38.360
Yeah, where we analyze the long-term behavior of numerical approximations

23:38.360 --> 23:42.940
in relation to the deep geometric and dynamical symmetries of the underlying differential equations.

23:43.480 --> 23:46.860
Things like symplectic integrators for Hamiltonian systems.

23:47.460 --> 23:54.180
But learned AI models are overwhelmingly evaluated on snapshot forecast error at a fixed, short lead time.

23:54.880 --> 23:58.780
They optimize for minimizing the pixel-wise mean squared error at hour 24.

23:59.420 --> 24:02.080
They aren't optimizing for the conservation of mass or energy.

24:02.080 --> 24:06.180
Which brings us to the core mechanical flaw of the entire architecture.

24:06.840 --> 24:11.100
If the local 576-kilometer tile is trained to be highly accurate,

24:11.600 --> 24:14.720
why do the conservation budgets break down so catastrophically

24:14.720 --> 24:17.860
when those tiles are stitched together to form the global state?

24:18.380 --> 24:21.640
We have to look at the exact mathematics of the assembly step.

24:22.000 --> 24:24.720
Right, because this is the mathematical heart of Flaksian's critique.

24:25.140 --> 24:26.460
Composition is not free.

24:26.760 --> 24:27.220
Not at all.

24:27.220 --> 24:34.600
We have to analyze the specific operator Strata uses to assemble the global step from the overlapping local tiles.

24:35.380 --> 24:39.180
They utilize a technique known as a partition-of-unity blending operator.

24:39.840 --> 24:41.600
Okay, here's where it gets really interesting.

24:41.740 --> 24:45.160
Because the tiles aren't just placed edge-to-edge on a grid like a checkerboard.

24:45.340 --> 24:47.040
They overlap significantly.

24:47.040 --> 24:49.540
Right, and where two or more tiles overlap,

24:50.040 --> 24:53.940
this system computes a spatially weighted average of their independent predictions

24:53.940 --> 24:56.640
to ensure a smooth transition across the boundary.

24:57.260 --> 25:03.080
A partition of unity ensures that the sum of the spatial weights at any point on the manifold equals exactly one.

25:03.500 --> 25:07.760
It guarantees analytical smoothness and continuity across the tile boundaries.

25:08.200 --> 25:10.040
But, and this is the huge but,

25:10.040 --> 25:13.660
Flaksian proves that while it guarantees smoothness,

25:14.060 --> 25:16.320
it completely destroys conservation.

25:17.220 --> 25:20.320
To understand how a smooth blend mathematically deletes mass,

25:20.400 --> 25:23.580
we have to look at the residual equation Flaksian formulates.

25:23.880 --> 25:26.500
Yeah, let's assume an idealized scenario first.

25:26.820 --> 25:29.840
Let's say every single tile is a perfect local operator.

25:30.460 --> 25:32.980
It is locally conservative in flux form.

25:33.420 --> 25:36.300
Within its 576 kilometer boundary,

25:36.640 --> 25:38.940
it perfectly tracks the advection of mass.

25:38.940 --> 25:43.120
But when you blend those overlapping perfectly conservative tiles together

25:43.120 --> 25:45.000
to compute the global state update,

25:45.580 --> 25:49.400
the blending operation introduces an explicit non-physical residual,

25:49.680 --> 25:51.600
which Flaksian denotes as R.

25:52.080 --> 25:54.640
The residual R is defined by the equation

25:54.640 --> 26:00.880
R equals the gradient of WI dot the quantity FI minus F bar.

26:01.400 --> 26:04.780
So the error introduced into the system is the dot product

26:04.780 --> 26:07.060
of the spatial gradient of the blending weights

26:07.060 --> 26:11.320
and the difference between the local tiles predicted flux, FI,

26:11.740 --> 26:14.380
and the blended average flux, F bar.

26:14.860 --> 26:16.620
This equation is profound.

26:17.120 --> 26:21.600
It pinpoints exactly where and why the physical laws break down.

26:21.700 --> 26:22.280
Walk us through it.

26:22.800 --> 26:25.240
The spatial gradient of the weight represents

26:25.240 --> 26:29.020
how steeply the blending function is changing across the overlap region.

26:29.020 --> 26:31.980
It is steepest at the very edges of the tiles.

26:33.060 --> 26:34.840
So if we read the dot product functionally,

26:34.920 --> 26:37.540
it means the artificial creation or destruction of mass

26:37.540 --> 26:39.840
is maximized exactly where the blending weights

26:39.840 --> 26:41.240
are changing most aggressively,

26:41.800 --> 26:44.060
provided that the overlapping tiles disagree

26:44.060 --> 26:46.720
on the physical flux at that specific location.

26:46.980 --> 26:47.340
Exactly.

26:47.960 --> 26:50.920
Even if the disagreement that FI minus F bar

26:50.920 --> 26:52.400
is incredibly small,

26:53.060 --> 26:55.800
multiplying it by the steep gradient of the boundary transition

26:55.800 --> 26:58.820
forces a non-zero residual R.

26:59.700 --> 27:01.580
The divergence of the blended flux

27:01.580 --> 27:03.700
no longer equals the physical change in mass.

27:04.220 --> 27:07.100
And Flyxion constructs a minimal analytic counterexample

27:07.100 --> 27:09.440
on a 1D periodic interval

27:09.440 --> 27:10.980
to demonstrate this unequivocally.

27:11.140 --> 27:12.460
It is a great thought experiment.

27:12.660 --> 27:12.840
Yeah.

27:13.600 --> 27:15.320
Imagine a simple closed loop.

27:15.700 --> 27:17.460
Tile 1 covers one part of the loop

27:17.460 --> 27:19.040
and predicts a localized flux

27:19.040 --> 27:20.520
that takes the form of a sine wave.

27:20.740 --> 27:21.000
Right.

27:21.000 --> 27:22.080
Mass moves forward.

27:22.400 --> 27:23.180
Mass moves backward.

27:23.180 --> 27:24.960
But over the domain of the sine wave,

27:25.340 --> 27:27.500
the net change in mass is perfectly zero.

27:27.860 --> 27:28.540
It is conservative.

27:29.460 --> 27:29.580
Okay.

27:29.720 --> 27:31.920
Now tile 2 overlaps with tile 1.

27:32.160 --> 27:34.660
But tile 2 predicts a flat flux of zero.

27:34.980 --> 27:35.660
Nothing moves.

27:36.120 --> 27:38.080
Again, perfectly conservative on its own.

27:38.520 --> 27:40.860
But when you apply the partition of unity blending

27:40.860 --> 27:41.960
to stitch them together,

27:42.680 --> 27:44.380
you are averaging a sine wave

27:44.380 --> 27:46.660
and a flat line across a spatial gradient.

27:47.160 --> 27:49.420
And the integral of that blended update

27:49.420 --> 27:51.460
across the entire periodic domain

27:51.460 --> 27:52.620
is no longer zero.

27:53.120 --> 27:55.060
The total mass of the closed system shifts.

27:55.620 --> 27:57.120
You have mathematically composed

27:57.120 --> 27:59.360
two perfectly conservative operators

27:59.360 --> 28:01.820
into a globally non-conservative one.

28:02.020 --> 28:03.380
The smooth blending function

28:03.380 --> 28:05.860
acts as an artificial sink or source,

28:06.040 --> 28:07.440
bleeding mass and energy

28:07.440 --> 28:08.340
out of the simulation

28:08.340 --> 28:09.880
at every single time step.

28:10.060 --> 28:11.180
And when you extrapolate

28:11.180 --> 28:12.380
that mathematical reality

28:12.380 --> 28:14.680
from a simple 1D sine wave

28:14.680 --> 28:17.340
to a 24-layer kilometer scale

28:17.340 --> 28:18.880
neural network operating across

28:18.880 --> 28:20.800
a global planetary manifold,

28:21.240 --> 28:22.640
the accumulated mass drift

28:22.640 --> 28:24.540
becomes structurally catastrophic.

28:24.540 --> 28:25.860
Which mathematically defines

28:25.860 --> 28:26.600
the difference between

28:26.600 --> 28:27.460
a weak continuation

28:27.460 --> 28:29.040
and a strong continuation.

28:29.640 --> 28:29.840
Yes.

28:30.620 --> 28:32.080
If we connect this to the bigger picture,

28:32.260 --> 28:35.360
strata relies entirely on weak continuation.

28:36.040 --> 28:37.820
It reconciles local predictions

28:37.820 --> 28:39.840
numerically after the fact

28:39.840 --> 28:41.760
by simply averaging them together

28:41.760 --> 28:42.860
to enforce smoothness.

28:42.860 --> 28:45.380
It's like, imagine two people mapping a room.

28:45.780 --> 28:48.140
Person A maps the left side perfectly.

28:48.360 --> 28:50.520
Person B maps the right side perfectly.

28:50.660 --> 28:52.180
But in the middle overlap,

28:52.300 --> 28:53.280
A says there's a couch

28:53.280 --> 28:54.840
and B says there's no couch.

28:55.080 --> 28:55.280
Right.

28:55.520 --> 28:56.860
If you just average them

28:56.860 --> 28:57.920
to make a smooth map,

28:58.000 --> 29:00.640
you end up with a semi-transparent ghost couch.

29:01.100 --> 29:01.840
The map is smooth,

29:01.960 --> 29:03.740
but the physical mass of the couch is wrong.

29:04.300 --> 29:05.260
That is exactly it.

29:05.940 --> 29:07.480
A strong continuation demands

29:07.480 --> 29:08.340
that the local predictions

29:08.340 --> 29:09.600
explicitly satisfy

29:09.600 --> 29:11.320
physical compatibility conditions

29:11.320 --> 29:12.820
at their interfaces

29:12.820 --> 29:15.200
before any state update is calculated.

29:15.820 --> 29:17.440
Or that the assembly operator itself

29:17.440 --> 29:18.640
is mathematically proven

29:18.640 --> 29:20.320
to conserve the relevant quantities.

29:21.140 --> 29:22.820
Averaging incompatible physical states

29:22.820 --> 29:25.200
does not magically render them compatible.

29:25.440 --> 29:26.140
No, it doesn't.

29:26.620 --> 29:28.720
And Flyxion extends this exact logic

29:28.720 --> 29:30.460
to the geometric constraints

29:30.460 --> 29:31.480
of the Earth as well.

29:32.040 --> 29:34.140
They discuss parallel transport

29:34.140 --> 29:35.560
on a spherical manifold.

29:35.740 --> 29:36.820
The holonomy, right.

29:36.960 --> 29:37.120
Right.

29:37.480 --> 29:38.660
When you transport a vector

29:38.660 --> 29:39.860
around a closed loop

29:39.860 --> 29:40.720
on a curved surface,

29:41.400 --> 29:42.820
the geometry of the sphere

29:42.820 --> 29:44.120
dictates that the vector

29:44.120 --> 29:45.620
will point in a different direction

29:45.620 --> 29:47.260
when it returns to its origin.

29:47.860 --> 29:49.120
This is holonomy.

29:49.440 --> 29:51.640
So if an AI is genuinely learning

29:51.640 --> 29:52.580
the physics of the planet,

29:52.880 --> 29:54.500
it must inherently respect

29:54.500 --> 29:55.980
the true holonomy of the sphere.

29:56.580 --> 29:58.860
But because Strata projects everything

29:58.860 --> 30:00.860
onto local flat tangent planes

30:00.860 --> 30:03.300
and relies on weak continuation

30:03.300 --> 30:04.320
to stitch them together,

30:05.020 --> 30:06.580
there is no geometric guarantee

30:06.580 --> 30:08.920
that a planetary wave propagating

30:08.920 --> 30:09.760
around the globe

30:09.760 --> 30:11.800
will maintain its physical integrity.

30:12.580 --> 30:13.920
The AI might just be memorizing

30:13.920 --> 30:15.140
local coordinate transitions

30:15.140 --> 30:16.580
that appear highly accurate

30:16.580 --> 30:18.920
within the 576-kilometer tile,

30:19.100 --> 30:20.840
but they just fail to close globally

30:20.840 --> 30:23.220
when integrated across the curve manifold.

30:23.440 --> 30:23.780
Exactly.

30:24.340 --> 30:26.240
So if the assembly step

30:26.240 --> 30:28.220
fundamentally destroys conservation,

30:28.960 --> 30:30.360
and the coordinate transformations

30:30.360 --> 30:32.480
lack guaranteed geometric closure,

30:32.780 --> 30:34.200
we are forced to rethink

30:34.200 --> 30:35.460
how we judge the output.

30:35.460 --> 30:37.380
We cannot simply evaluate

30:37.380 --> 30:38.840
the 24-hour snapshot

30:38.840 --> 30:40.880
and declare the model highly accurate

30:40.880 --> 30:42.760
because the clouds look realistic.

30:43.200 --> 30:43.320
No.

30:43.760 --> 30:45.540
Because stitching destroys conservation,

30:46.080 --> 30:47.420
we can't just look at snapshots

30:47.420 --> 30:47.900
of the weather

30:47.900 --> 30:49.020
to see if the model works.

30:49.120 --> 30:50.660
We have to look at the whole history.

30:50.920 --> 30:52.880
We have to examine the entire trajectory.

30:53.660 --> 30:54.920
Flyxion formalizes this

30:54.920 --> 30:56.320
by drawing a sharp line

30:56.320 --> 30:57.820
between plausible states

30:57.820 --> 30:59.240
and admissible histories.

30:59.580 --> 31:00.800
A plausible state simply means

31:00.800 --> 31:02.800
that a single isolated frame

31:02.800 --> 31:03.440
of the simulation

31:03.440 --> 31:05.620
resides on the correct atmospheric manifold.

31:06.360 --> 31:07.680
If you evaluate the temperature,

31:07.980 --> 31:09.160
pressure, and humidity fields

31:09.160 --> 31:10.100
at hour 24,

31:10.920 --> 31:12.040
they statistically resemble

31:12.040 --> 31:13.320
a real atmospheric state.

31:13.860 --> 31:15.820
But an admissible history

31:15.820 --> 31:17.860
requires that the sequential transitions

31:17.860 --> 31:18.900
between those states

31:18.900 --> 31:21.460
strictly obey the governing dynamical laws.

31:21.980 --> 31:22.880
You can have a sequence

31:22.880 --> 31:24.720
of perfectly plausible states

31:24.720 --> 31:27.180
that form a completely inadmissible history.

31:27.180 --> 31:28.640
To conceptualize this,

31:29.360 --> 31:30.640
consider a financial ledger.

31:31.540 --> 31:32.300
You take a snapshot

31:32.300 --> 31:33.760
of an account balance on Monday

31:33.760 --> 31:34.620
and it reads $100.

31:35.540 --> 31:37.160
You take another snapshot on Tuesday

31:37.160 --> 31:38.360
and it reads $100.

31:39.020 --> 31:40.780
Both states are highly plausible

31:40.780 --> 31:41.640
and appear stable.

31:41.760 --> 31:43.100
Looks perfectly fine, right?

31:43.300 --> 31:44.520
But the ledger's history

31:44.520 --> 31:45.920
between those snapshots

31:45.920 --> 31:47.920
might reveal that $10 million

31:47.920 --> 31:49.500
was artificially injected

31:49.500 --> 31:50.360
into the account

31:50.360 --> 31:51.940
to cover a massive,

31:52.160 --> 31:53.100
unauthorized withdrawal,

31:53.460 --> 31:55.400
and then another artificial intervention

31:55.400 --> 31:56.440
removed the excess

31:56.440 --> 31:58.620
to return the balance to $100.

31:59.120 --> 32:00.440
The individual snapshots

32:00.440 --> 32:02.020
hid massive volatility.

32:02.400 --> 32:02.620
Yes.

32:03.440 --> 32:04.520
The absolute violation

32:04.520 --> 32:05.420
of accounting rules,

32:05.920 --> 32:07.780
that is the exact analog

32:07.780 --> 32:10.040
for how these AI emulators function

32:10.040 --> 32:11.700
when relying on post-hoc clipping

32:11.700 --> 32:12.620
and spectral filtering.

32:13.040 --> 32:14.300
The hourly snapshots

32:14.300 --> 32:16.000
present a plausible sequence

32:16.000 --> 32:16.800
of weather patterns.

32:17.340 --> 32:18.300
But the silent,

32:18.560 --> 32:20.280
continuous artificial injection

32:20.280 --> 32:21.680
and deletion of water mass

32:21.680 --> 32:23.300
via negative humidity clipping,

32:23.940 --> 32:25.380
that renders the temporal

32:25.380 --> 32:27.160
evolution physically impossible.

32:27.160 --> 32:29.440
It is an inadmissible history.

32:30.100 --> 32:30.560
And this raises

32:30.560 --> 32:31.640
an important question.

32:31.980 --> 32:33.160
How much of AI's success

32:33.160 --> 32:33.880
is just creating

32:33.880 --> 32:35.040
plausible snapshots

32:35.040 --> 32:36.520
versus actually understanding

32:36.520 --> 32:37.700
the underlying laws?

32:38.500 --> 32:39.960
This demands a profound shift

32:39.960 --> 32:41.400
in how we audit these systems.

32:41.840 --> 32:43.180
So what does Fleijen propose?

32:44.140 --> 32:45.780
They argue that an honest description

32:45.780 --> 32:47.040
of an AI's trajectory

32:47.040 --> 32:48.100
cannot just present

32:48.100 --> 32:49.700
the final smoothed output.

32:50.260 --> 32:51.740
The simulation must be auditable

32:51.740 --> 32:53.740
in the strict computer science

32:53.740 --> 32:55.960
sense of an append-only ledger.

32:55.960 --> 32:56.900
An audit trail.

32:57.240 --> 32:57.400
Right.

32:57.920 --> 32:59.360
Every time the neural network output

32:59.360 --> 32:59.820
is filtered,

33:00.020 --> 33:02.260
every time a negative precipitation value

33:02.260 --> 33:03.100
is clipped to zero,

33:03.480 --> 33:05.580
every time a non-conservative blend

33:05.580 --> 33:07.420
forces a residual mass error,

33:07.660 --> 33:09.460
it must be explicitly logged.

33:09.460 --> 33:11.560
Because if you hide the interventions,

33:11.920 --> 33:13.440
you obscure the true dynamics

33:13.440 --> 33:14.500
of the learned operator.

33:15.020 --> 33:15.900
The scientific community

33:15.900 --> 33:16.780
cannot evaluate

33:16.780 --> 33:17.840
the physical fidelity

33:17.840 --> 33:18.680
of an emulator

33:18.680 --> 33:19.980
if the internal band-aids

33:19.980 --> 33:21.340
holding the simulation together

33:21.340 --> 33:22.740
are executed silently.

33:23.240 --> 33:23.800
Precisely.

33:24.140 --> 33:25.840
And to address this structurally

33:25.840 --> 33:27.580
rather than just diagnosing it,

33:28.140 --> 33:29.920
Fleijen proposes a radical redesign

33:29.920 --> 33:31.340
of the objective function

33:31.340 --> 33:32.300
used during training.

33:32.560 --> 33:33.400
Instead of just using

33:33.400 --> 33:34.540
a standard loss function

33:34.540 --> 33:36.200
that penalizes the prediction error

33:36.200 --> 33:37.500
against the reference snapshot,

33:38.060 --> 33:38.860
Fleijen introduces

33:38.860 --> 33:40.780
a composite objective function.

33:41.020 --> 33:41.260
Right.

33:41.560 --> 33:42.720
L equals Lpred

33:42.720 --> 33:45.140
plus lambda c times L constraint

33:45.140 --> 33:47.460
plus lambda h times L history.

33:47.760 --> 33:48.840
Okay, let's break that down.

33:49.020 --> 33:49.140
Right.

33:49.580 --> 33:50.760
Lpred is the standard

33:50.760 --> 33:52.040
snapshot prediction error.

33:52.320 --> 33:52.560
Right.

33:52.740 --> 33:54.080
And L constraint

33:54.080 --> 33:55.720
explicitly penalizes

33:55.720 --> 33:57.000
the violation of localized

33:57.000 --> 33:58.140
physical rules

33:58.140 --> 33:59.320
during the forward pass.

33:59.860 --> 34:01.400
But the critical innovation

34:01.400 --> 34:02.820
is L history.

34:03.320 --> 34:05.180
The accumulated history deviations.

34:05.640 --> 34:05.940
Yes.

34:05.940 --> 34:07.540
This term penalizes

34:07.540 --> 34:08.780
accumulated deviations

34:08.780 --> 34:09.860
over the long-term

34:09.860 --> 34:11.320
temporal trajectory

34:11.320 --> 34:13.220
that no single snapshot

34:13.220 --> 34:14.380
could possibly reveal.

34:15.160 --> 34:16.720
It mathematically forces

34:16.720 --> 34:17.600
the neural network

34:17.600 --> 34:19.060
to optimize for the conservation

34:19.060 --> 34:20.540
of global budgets

34:20.540 --> 34:21.560
across time.

34:21.560 --> 34:23.180
It forces the AI

34:23.180 --> 34:24.140
to balance the ledger

34:24.140 --> 34:25.540
rather than just fabricating

34:25.540 --> 34:26.980
a plausible ending balance.

34:27.220 --> 34:27.620
Exactly.

34:28.420 --> 34:29.740
But even if we implement

34:29.740 --> 34:31.220
this rigorous objective function,

34:31.720 --> 34:33.420
how do we force an AI

34:33.420 --> 34:36.060
to care about the underlying laws?

34:36.580 --> 34:37.560
How do we ensure

34:37.560 --> 34:38.500
it is extracting

34:38.500 --> 34:40.080
the necessary causal variables

34:40.080 --> 34:41.020
from the training data

34:41.020 --> 34:41.780
in the first place?

34:41.780 --> 34:42.940
Which is tricky

34:42.940 --> 34:44.200
because the strata engineers

34:44.200 --> 34:45.500
justified their massive

34:45.500 --> 34:46.860
computational expenditure

34:46.860 --> 34:48.220
by pointing to

34:48.220 --> 34:49.360
the differential entropy

34:49.360 --> 34:50.640
of high-resolution

34:50.640 --> 34:51.700
atmospheric states.

34:52.120 --> 34:53.220
They argued that

34:53.220 --> 34:54.980
resolving kilometer-scale

34:54.980 --> 34:55.440
turbulence

34:55.440 --> 34:56.480
requires retaining

34:56.480 --> 34:57.440
a massive amount

34:57.440 --> 34:58.100
of information.

34:58.780 --> 34:59.480
And Flikassian

34:59.480 --> 35:00.940
dismantles this justification

35:00.940 --> 35:01.920
by exposing

35:01.920 --> 35:03.140
a fundamental confusion

35:03.140 --> 35:04.900
within the AI community.

35:05.280 --> 35:06.040
The confusion

35:06.040 --> 35:07.620
between marginal entropy

35:07.620 --> 35:09.240
and conditional entropy.

35:09.240 --> 35:10.660
Let's define the boundary

35:10.660 --> 35:11.940
between those two concepts.

35:12.320 --> 35:13.120
Marginal entropy,

35:13.660 --> 35:14.320
H of X,

35:14.480 --> 35:15.520
is simply a measure

35:15.520 --> 35:17.120
of how much raw information

35:17.120 --> 35:17.820
or complexity

35:17.820 --> 35:18.880
is present

35:18.880 --> 35:20.260
in a single snapshot

35:20.260 --> 35:20.840
of a system.

35:21.400 --> 35:22.620
A highly turbulent,

35:23.040 --> 35:24.480
high-resolution fluid field

35:24.480 --> 35:26.680
has massive marginal entropy.

35:26.820 --> 35:28.300
It requires a huge number

35:28.300 --> 35:28.740
of bits

35:28.740 --> 35:29.880
to describe visually.

35:29.880 --> 35:31.460
But conditional entropy,

35:32.040 --> 35:33.800
H of X at time T

35:33.800 --> 35:34.740
plus delta T

35:34.740 --> 35:36.200
given X at time T

35:36.200 --> 35:37.540
and boundary conditions B

35:37.540 --> 35:38.380
at time T,

35:38.940 --> 35:39.620
well that measures

35:39.620 --> 35:40.620
how much uncertainty

35:40.620 --> 35:42.380
remains about the future state

35:42.380 --> 35:43.700
given that you perfectly

35:43.700 --> 35:44.920
know the current state.

35:45.120 --> 35:45.500
Exactly.

35:46.000 --> 35:47.720
If the system strictly obeys

35:47.720 --> 35:49.620
deterministic physical laws,

35:50.280 --> 35:51.380
the conditional entropy

35:51.380 --> 35:52.900
can be incredibly low

35:52.900 --> 35:54.340
even if the marginal entropy

35:54.340 --> 35:55.360
is extremely high.

35:55.760 --> 35:57.440
A complex visual field

35:57.440 --> 35:58.840
can be highly predictable.

35:58.840 --> 36:00.060
And conversely,

36:00.220 --> 36:01.100
a system that appears

36:01.100 --> 36:02.000
visually simple,

36:02.300 --> 36:03.340
low marginal entropy,

36:03.900 --> 36:04.420
can possess

36:04.420 --> 36:05.680
high conditional entropy

36:05.680 --> 36:06.560
if the variables

36:06.560 --> 36:07.600
required to predict

36:07.600 --> 36:08.160
its evolution

36:08.160 --> 36:09.200
are not captured

36:09.200 --> 36:10.500
by the state representation.

36:10.960 --> 36:11.180
Right.

36:11.640 --> 36:13.020
And Flyxion points out

36:13.020 --> 36:14.060
that AI models

36:14.060 --> 36:15.020
optimized solely

36:15.020 --> 36:16.840
for pixel-wise reconstruction

36:16.840 --> 36:18.660
often prioritize

36:18.660 --> 36:19.560
retaining features

36:19.560 --> 36:20.960
with high marginal entropy

36:20.960 --> 36:22.540
to make the image

36:22.540 --> 36:23.180
look sharp

36:23.180 --> 36:25.060
while silently discarding

36:25.060 --> 36:25.980
features with low

36:25.980 --> 36:26.760
marginal entropy

36:26.760 --> 36:27.880
that are actually

36:27.880 --> 36:29.140
the causal drivers

36:29.140 --> 36:29.860
of the physics.

36:30.320 --> 36:31.160
The prime example

36:31.160 --> 36:32.660
being localized gradients.

36:33.180 --> 36:34.360
Imagine a weather state

36:34.360 --> 36:36.440
where the overall visual field

36:36.440 --> 36:37.520
is relatively calm,

36:37.920 --> 36:39.660
but there is a microscopically

36:39.660 --> 36:41.100
sharp moisture gradient,

36:41.440 --> 36:42.940
a very tight boundary

36:42.940 --> 36:44.080
between dry air

36:44.080 --> 36:45.200
and saturated air.

36:45.580 --> 36:46.820
That localized gradient

36:46.820 --> 36:47.600
might represent

36:47.600 --> 36:48.440
a tiny fraction

36:48.440 --> 36:49.600
of the overall pixel data,

36:50.000 --> 36:51.720
but it is the exact

36:51.720 --> 36:52.440
causal trigger

36:52.440 --> 36:53.260
that will initiate

36:53.260 --> 36:54.980
a massive convective storm cell

36:54.980 --> 36:55.760
three hours later.

36:55.760 --> 36:57.460
If the neural network

36:57.460 --> 36:59.280
compresses the state representation

36:59.280 --> 37:00.240
and blurs

37:00.240 --> 37:01.480
that localized gradient

37:01.480 --> 37:03.100
in favor of preserving

37:03.100 --> 37:04.220
the broader

37:04.220 --> 37:06.100
high entropy background textures,

37:06.620 --> 37:08.180
it maintains a low prediction error

37:08.180 --> 37:09.360
on the immediate snapshot,

37:09.680 --> 37:11.260
but entirely destroys

37:11.260 --> 37:12.260
its ability to predict

37:12.260 --> 37:13.340
the physical evolution

37:13.340 --> 37:13.980
of the system.

37:14.440 --> 37:15.640
It is prioritizing

37:15.640 --> 37:16.900
the aesthetic resolution

37:16.900 --> 37:18.040
of the present state

37:18.040 --> 37:19.780
over the physical causality

37:19.780 --> 37:21.480
required for the future state.

37:21.480 --> 37:22.620
To correct this,

37:23.140 --> 37:24.080
Flyxion points toward

37:24.080 --> 37:25.320
a constrained rate

37:25.320 --> 37:26.360
distortion framework.

37:26.980 --> 37:28.340
The goal of the neural representation

37:28.340 --> 37:30.000
shouldn't be to compress the data

37:30.000 --> 37:30.680
while minimizing

37:30.680 --> 37:31.600
visual distortion.

37:32.260 --> 37:33.100
The goal must be

37:33.100 --> 37:33.620
to determine

37:33.620 --> 37:35.360
the absolute minimum information

37:35.360 --> 37:36.700
the model must retain

37:36.700 --> 37:37.780
to preserve

37:37.780 --> 37:39.600
an admissible physical evolution.

37:39.900 --> 37:41.020
It completely reframes

37:41.020 --> 37:41.820
how we evaluate

37:41.820 --> 37:43.180
latent space representations

37:43.180 --> 37:44.180
in physical AI,

37:44.820 --> 37:45.620
and Flyxion grounds

37:45.620 --> 37:47.420
this entire theoretical critique

37:47.420 --> 37:48.300
in a concrete,

37:48.600 --> 37:49.540
mathematically provable

37:49.540 --> 37:50.220
challenge to the

37:50.220 --> 37:51.180
machine learning community.

37:51.480 --> 37:52.200
The conservative

37:52.200 --> 37:53.520
advection experiment.

37:53.700 --> 37:54.720
Yeah, they conclude the essay

37:54.720 --> 37:55.640
by essentially assigning

37:55.640 --> 37:57.260
homework to the AI community.

37:57.480 --> 37:58.380
Literal homework, yeah.

37:59.020 --> 37:59.780
Before we attempt

37:59.780 --> 38:00.280
to emulate

38:00.280 --> 38:01.040
century-scale

38:01.040 --> 38:02.200
global climate dynamics

38:02.200 --> 38:03.500
on a spherical manifold,

38:04.140 --> 38:04.840
Flyxion demands

38:04.840 --> 38:05.620
that the architecture

38:05.620 --> 38:06.320
prove itself

38:06.320 --> 38:08.040
on a vastly simplified domain

38:08.040 --> 38:09.620
where the exact

38:09.620 --> 38:10.520
mathematical answers

38:10.520 --> 38:11.080
are known.

38:11.480 --> 38:12.600
The experiment involves

38:12.600 --> 38:14.120
modeling conservative advection

38:14.120 --> 38:15.800
using finite volume methods

38:15.800 --> 38:17.240
on a simple 2D

38:17.240 --> 38:18.540
periodic square domain.

38:18.880 --> 38:19.980
The setup requires

38:19.980 --> 38:20.960
training a localized

38:20.960 --> 38:22.360
neural update operator

38:22.360 --> 38:23.640
on spatial tiles,

38:24.040 --> 38:24.960
exactly mirroring

38:24.960 --> 38:25.900
the Strata approach.

38:26.400 --> 38:27.300
But the critical test

38:27.300 --> 38:28.660
evaluates three distinct

38:28.660 --> 38:29.760
methods for assembling

38:29.760 --> 38:30.740
those local tiles

38:30.740 --> 38:31.820
into a global state.

38:32.220 --> 38:33.060
Okay, let's go through

38:33.060 --> 38:33.640
the three methods.

38:33.800 --> 38:34.620
Method one is

38:34.620 --> 38:35.520
increment blending.

38:35.840 --> 38:36.080
Right.

38:36.560 --> 38:37.480
This is the standard

38:37.480 --> 38:38.880
partition of unity approach

38:38.880 --> 38:39.700
used by Strata,

38:40.200 --> 38:41.640
averaging the predicted updates

38:41.640 --> 38:42.860
in the overlap region.

38:43.260 --> 38:44.280
Method two is

38:44.280 --> 38:45.040
flux blending.

38:45.380 --> 38:46.440
This involves blending

38:46.440 --> 38:47.900
the predicted fluxes first

38:47.900 --> 38:49.160
and then calculating

38:49.160 --> 38:49.820
the divergence

38:49.820 --> 38:50.880
of that blended flux

38:50.880 --> 38:52.080
to update the state.

38:52.080 --> 38:53.280
And method three

38:53.280 --> 38:55.280
is shared interface fluxes.

38:55.660 --> 38:56.540
This is the implementation

38:56.540 --> 38:57.660
of strong continuation.

38:58.040 --> 38:58.320
Exactly.

38:58.960 --> 39:00.820
In a shared interface flux assembly,

39:01.280 --> 39:02.040
adjacent tiles

39:02.040 --> 39:03.540
do not operate independently

39:03.540 --> 39:05.180
and average their results later.

39:05.760 --> 39:06.600
The architecture

39:06.600 --> 39:07.880
explicitly forces

39:07.880 --> 39:08.720
adjacent tiles

39:08.720 --> 39:09.300
to share

39:09.300 --> 39:10.600
and mutually cancel

39:10.600 --> 39:11.960
their numerical fluxes

39:11.960 --> 39:13.760
precisely at the boundary interface.

39:13.760 --> 39:15.820
So if the numerical scheme

39:15.820 --> 39:16.740
on tile A

39:16.740 --> 39:18.020
dictates that a specific

39:18.020 --> 39:18.940
mass flux

39:18.940 --> 39:20.240
exits its right boundary,

39:20.820 --> 39:22.340
the exact same mass flux

39:22.340 --> 39:23.340
must simultaneously

39:23.340 --> 39:25.040
enter the left boundary

39:25.040 --> 39:25.740
of tile B.

39:25.960 --> 39:26.180
Yes.

39:27.020 --> 39:27.920
The boundary fluxes

39:27.920 --> 39:29.540
must be perfectly reconciled

39:29.540 --> 39:30.520
before the simulation

39:30.520 --> 39:31.480
is allowed to advance

39:31.480 --> 39:32.420
to the next time step.

39:32.840 --> 39:34.220
They have to mathematically

39:34.220 --> 39:36.180
agree on the physical transition

39:36.180 --> 39:37.060
at the interface.

39:37.520 --> 39:38.760
The objective of the experiment

39:38.760 --> 39:40.260
is to empirically demonstrate

39:40.260 --> 39:41.740
that enforcing this exact

39:41.740 --> 39:42.680
local compatibility

39:42.680 --> 39:44.680
via shared interface fluxes

39:44.680 --> 39:46.600
is what actually guarantees

39:46.600 --> 39:48.180
long horizon physical fidelity.

39:49.060 --> 39:49.920
Flyxion is positing

39:49.920 --> 39:51.120
that until an architecture

39:51.120 --> 39:53.280
can successfully conserve mass

39:53.280 --> 39:53.960
on a simple,

39:54.420 --> 39:55.500
flat, periodic square

39:55.500 --> 39:56.620
using strict

39:56.620 --> 39:57.680
interface reconciliation,

39:58.260 --> 39:59.720
any claims of physical fidelity

39:59.720 --> 40:01.380
on a complex spherical manifold

40:01.380 --> 40:03.380
are epistemically void.

40:03.720 --> 40:05.920
It is a phenomenal reality check

40:05.920 --> 40:07.240
for the entire field

40:07.240 --> 40:08.520
of neural Earth system modeling.

40:09.000 --> 40:10.840
We've traversed an immense amount

40:10.840 --> 40:11.860
of technical terrain today,

40:11.860 --> 40:12.860
so let's distill

40:12.860 --> 40:14.060
the fundamental insights.

40:14.880 --> 40:15.980
We started by examining

40:15.980 --> 40:17.020
the undeniable

40:17.020 --> 40:18.240
computational brilliance

40:18.240 --> 40:18.720
of strata.

40:19.080 --> 40:20.580
The ability to utilize

40:20.580 --> 40:21.660
transformer attention

40:21.660 --> 40:24.680
on local 576-kilometer tiles

40:24.680 --> 40:27.940
to achieve a 62-fold acceleration

40:27.940 --> 40:30.020
over traditional numerical models.

40:30.520 --> 40:30.840
Right.

40:31.100 --> 40:32.720
And then we uncovered

40:32.720 --> 40:33.940
the emulation illusion.

40:34.740 --> 40:35.680
Strata is not learning

40:35.680 --> 40:36.700
atmospheric physics.

40:37.000 --> 40:38.640
It is curve-fitting the biases

40:38.640 --> 40:40.660
of a parameterized reference model

40:40.660 --> 40:42.920
based on an effective sample size

40:42.920 --> 40:44.140
that is severely constrained

40:44.140 --> 40:44.960
by the spatial

40:44.960 --> 40:46.580
and temporal autocorrelation

40:46.580 --> 40:48.300
of a mere 17 days of data.

40:48.300 --> 40:50.100
We analyzed how this leads

40:50.100 --> 40:51.340
to convincing weather

40:51.340 --> 40:53.040
in the wrong world.

40:53.820 --> 40:55.540
The AI accurately generates

40:55.540 --> 40:57.480
local convective clouds

40:57.480 --> 40:59.260
while systematically suppressing

40:59.260 --> 41:00.320
the spectral power

41:00.320 --> 41:01.760
of synoptic scale waves

41:01.760 --> 41:03.420
and bleeding 20%

41:03.420 --> 41:05.080
of the global precipitation budget.

41:05.080 --> 41:07.620
We unpacked why post-hoc interventions

41:07.620 --> 41:09.240
like clipping negative humidity

41:09.240 --> 41:10.920
or spectrally filtering

41:10.920 --> 41:11.920
vertical velocity

41:11.920 --> 41:13.660
satisfy numerical constraints

41:13.660 --> 41:15.340
but permanently violate

41:15.340 --> 41:16.140
physical fidelity

41:16.140 --> 41:17.740
and global conservation laws.

41:17.900 --> 41:19.420
And at the mathematical core,

41:19.800 --> 41:21.180
we dissected the partition

41:21.180 --> 41:22.760
of unity blending operator.

41:23.340 --> 41:24.940
We explored the residual equation,

41:25.140 --> 41:26.160
demonstrating exactly

41:26.160 --> 41:27.180
how the spatial gradient

41:27.180 --> 41:28.180
of the blending weights

41:28.180 --> 41:29.980
interacts with localized

41:29.980 --> 41:31.100
flux discrepancies

41:31.100 --> 41:33.200
to act as an artificial source

41:33.200 --> 41:34.060
or sink.

41:34.060 --> 41:35.740
Mathematically proving

41:35.740 --> 41:36.720
that the composition

41:36.720 --> 41:38.620
of overlapping local operators

41:38.620 --> 41:40.860
is inherently non-conservative,

41:40.860 --> 41:43.140
which fundamentally redefines

41:43.140 --> 41:44.540
our criteria for success,

41:45.080 --> 41:45.920
forcing the transition

41:45.920 --> 41:46.720
from evaluating

41:46.720 --> 41:48.260
plausible snapshot states

41:48.260 --> 41:49.220
to demanding

41:49.220 --> 41:51.100
strictly admissible histories

41:51.100 --> 41:52.580
governed by causal,

41:53.040 --> 41:53.920
localized gradients

41:53.920 --> 41:55.120
rather than marginal

41:55.120 --> 41:56.000
visual entropy.

41:56.340 --> 41:57.580
The ultimate takeaway

41:57.580 --> 41:59.760
is that local prediction accuracy

41:59.760 --> 42:01.620
combined with numerical smoothing

42:01.620 --> 42:03.460
does not yield global

42:03.460 --> 42:04.580
dynamical fidelity.

42:05.480 --> 42:06.440
Numerical stability

42:06.440 --> 42:07.620
does not equal

42:07.620 --> 42:08.880
physical correctness.

42:09.880 --> 42:11.020
Without structural guarantees

42:11.020 --> 42:12.240
of strong continuation,

42:12.840 --> 42:15.120
the manifold will invariably collapse.

42:15.120 --> 42:17.820
And that leads to a critical final thought

42:17.820 --> 42:18.920
for you to carry forward,

42:19.340 --> 42:20.300
well beyond the realm

42:20.300 --> 42:21.400
of atmospheric physics.

42:21.860 --> 42:24.080
As Flyxion makes explicitly clear,

42:24.640 --> 42:26.240
this mathematical vulnerability

42:26.240 --> 42:28.680
exists in any highly complex

42:28.680 --> 42:29.720
AI architecture

42:29.720 --> 42:31.200
that attempts to compose

42:31.200 --> 42:32.140
macro-reality

42:32.140 --> 42:33.360
from micro-predictions.

42:33.680 --> 42:35.300
If the world's most advanced

42:35.300 --> 42:37.280
AI models can perfectly simulate

42:37.280 --> 42:38.580
local patches of weather

42:38.580 --> 42:40.640
but secretly bleed water

42:40.640 --> 42:41.180
and mass

42:41.180 --> 42:42.160
when stitched together,

42:42.960 --> 42:45.540
where else is local accuracy

42:45.540 --> 42:47.540
hiding global collapse

42:47.540 --> 42:48.160
in your world?

42:48.840 --> 42:50.480
When you look at economic forecasts,

42:50.800 --> 42:51.700
supply chain models,

42:51.800 --> 42:53.100
or even medical predictions,

42:53.700 --> 42:54.420
are you looking at

42:54.420 --> 42:56.040
a truly admissible history

42:56.040 --> 42:58.620
or just a smoothly blended patchwork

42:58.620 --> 43:00.180
of plausible snapshots?

43:00.840 --> 43:03.260
It is the defining epistemic question

43:03.260 --> 43:04.200
for the next generation

43:04.200 --> 43:05.360
of artificial intelligence.

43:05.820 --> 43:06.940
Thanks for diving deep

43:06.940 --> 43:07.480
with us today.

